{"cti_reasoning":"The state phase=3, req=1, ack=1, busy=1 satisfies both assertions (req and ack are high but busy is also high), yet it violates the relation because in phase 3 the requester must already have dropped req (R says req == (phase==1||phase==2)). With step low the machine sits in this state forever; when step finally rises the phase-3 branch clears busy and retires the transaction, but leaves ack set (the responder only drops ack when it observes !req) and never touches req, giving req=1, ack=1, busy=0 in the next state, which violates both assertions.","cti_state":[{"signal":"phase","value":"2'd3"},{"signal":"req","value":"1'b1"},{"signal":"ack","value":"1'b1"},{"signal":"busy","value":"1'b1"}],"invariants":["req == (phase == 2'd1 || phase == 2'd2)","ack == (phase == 2'd2 || phase == 2'd3)","busy == (phase != 2'd0)"],"verilog":"module fetch_handshake (
    input  wire       clk,
    input  wire       rst,
    input  wire       step,      // global enable: low => design idles
